{
  "title": "Voice Tool Guide",
  "story": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "2418ee93d7f5ee68",
      "text": "Everything about the [[Voice Tool]] that is not needed to use it: what the notes page looks like, the directives, the local door, public farms. The tool page itself keeps to use, storage and privacy."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "1a67f393f935c9c1",
      "text": "# Naming the Page"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "39ae57d07f2c2351",
      "text": "> A spoken answer lands on the page named by the question."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "07f87184712bea22",
      "text": "Beside the title, **a page it is waiting for** lists the red links on the page to the left \u2014 pages it links to that nobody has written yet. Choose one and the notes take that name; saving turns the red link blue, so no new link is needed. Write an **Open Questions** list as red question links, `- [ ] [[Who may open an event?]]`, each question its own item, with no page behind the link, and the tool offers each question as a title. The [[Plan Tool]]'s Answer link opens the Voice Tool with the question already chosen."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "89bfe9a63877a917",
      "text": "**Save page & link it** puts the `[[link]]` on the page to the left before its See section and copies it to the clipboard \u2014 drag the link beside the question it answers. Change the title for each question and a series of linked notes pages builds up. A title that already exists here is refused rather than overwritten. A recording picked up without its transcript \u2014 the farm refused it, or the network dropped \u2014 shows a **\u21bb Transcribe** button, so the audio is sent again rather than spoken again."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "f7430138f9ab609f",
      "text": "**Add to this page** puts the notes on the page to the left itself; a prompt whose header has `SECTION: Description` sends them under that heading, filling an unfilled card template there in place, and without one they go before the See section."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "97cfe15c68a02c3d",
      "text": "# The Page a Note Makes"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "92c0bcce7426d06b",
      "text": "> The answer first, the trail last."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "89ad9fd4b91a2403",
      "text": "The page starts with the summary, each point its own item, with no heading or date above it \u2014 the question is the title and the journal keeps the date. It ends with a **# See** section linking back to the page it answers and to the prompt that shaped the summary; when that prompt was read from the prompt wiki, saving the page forks that site in (a fork event first in the journal, which hands over no credit), so the prompt link leads there. The transcript follows the answer under its own heading. With *keep the audio* ticked, an audio player captioned with the title and date, and the folder's assets (the recording and its transcript .txt), take the transcript's place."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "cea0feab0a5aef79",
      "text": "# Directives"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "a1e4f1e8d7394598",
      "text": "> Lines in the item that change what the tool does."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "84081d7dc9ee8b3d",
      "text": "`TITLE: {{page}} Notes` sets the pattern the title starts from. Prompts come from a prompt wiki (**PROMPTS:**, default prompt.geek.fish), read after this site; **PROMPT:** `[[Title]]` chooses one first and **KINDS:** limits which kinds are offered. Fork a prompt page to this site and your copy is the one used \u2014 see [[Prompt Format]]. `DOOR: farm` sends every recording to the page's own farm; `DOOR: http://host:port` names a local door of your own. On a phone, **record or choose a file** opens the phone's own recorder \u2014 the browser only allows the microphone itself over https."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "17b1458bfc1638aa",
      "text": "# The Local Door"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "b32a112d1841b1dd",
      "text": "> The machine you are sitting at hears you first."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "521e8074a895e2d7",
      "text": "A recording is normally sent to the farm serving the page. When the browser is on a machine that runs a farm of its own \u2014 a laptop with the private farm up \u2014 the Voice Tool asks that machine first: it probes `http://localhost:4242/system/api.json` once per page load, and if a farm answers whose *hear* operation is gated on the place alone, the recording goes through that **local door** instead. Only the audio travels; the transcript comes back, and the page, the prompt, the summary and any kept audio stay with the farm the page lives on. The status line says where the words were heard \u2014 *transcribing on this Mac, with MacWhisper*, or *on the farm*."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "655d6f823c38e3e2",
      "text": "On a Mac the local farm hears with **MacWhisper** whenever that app is open, using the model the app has selected (Parakeet v3, say), and with faster-whisper when it is closed; the choice is made per recording, so opening the app changes the next one. The farm's `AUDIONOTE_ENGINE` can pin either engine; `AUDIONOTE_MACWHISPER_MODEL` names a model in MacWhisper's `engine:id` form, and `AUDIONOTE_MACWHISPER_PERSIST=1` also files each recording in MacWhisper's History."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "c4d60bc91b88d243",
      "text": "The door answers a page from elsewhere without a login there because *hear* changes no page and keeps nothing \u2014 the farm transcribes from its temporary folder and deletes the file. In the Farm plugin's terms the operation is declared `x-fedwiki-gate: place` and `x-fedwiki-cross-origin: true`. A public farm still refuses it, as it refuses every write it has not been told to allow."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "469edaae929ed0c8",
      "text": "# Public Sites"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "368814f7f00c5398",
      "text": "> A public farm has to say yes first."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "ed7f2d54ff67e605",
      "text": "On a farm whose sites are reachable from the open internet, the Voice Tool's three writes \u2014 hear, transcribe and summarise \u2014 are refused with **403 \"is not a private site, and this farm has not been asked to allow writes to public ones\"** until the person running the farm lists them. The Farm plugin asks two questions in order: is this farm willing to take API writes on public sites at all, and may this caller edit this site. The first is answered by the farm's environment, the second by the wiki's own login. A private farm behind a tailnet answers the first with `FARM_API_PRIVATE_SUFFIXES`; a public farm needs `FARM_API_PUBLIC_WRITES=audionote.hear,audionote.transcribe,audionote.summarise,farm.assetsUpload`. Every scope is needed: listing only `audionote.hear` lets the transcript through and fails at summarise, and *keep the audio* uploads through the Farm plugin's own `farm.assetsUpload`, which is refused until it is listed too. The blanket value `1` would open every page-changing operation on the farm, including table writes and index rebuilds, and should not be used."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "07d71d9651220fe2",
      "text": "# See"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "9a705362b99a4eb4",
      "text": "- [[Voice Tool]]\n- [[About Audio Note Plugin]]\n- [[Voice Input Plan]]\n- [[Prompt Format]]"
    }
  ],
